  "callNowAction": "Call Now",
  "pubertyHeroDesc": "Puberty is a natural transition from childhood into adulthood, characterized by physical, hormonal, and emotional changes that shape personal growth and health.",
  "pubertyNoteText": "Every adolescent experiences puberty at their own unique pace—practicing good hygiene, understanding bodily changes, and embracing self-confidence helps ensure a healthy transition.",
  "pubertySection1Title": "Understanding Puberty & Adolescence",
  "pubertySection1Subtitle": "The Natural Transition to Adulthood",
  "pubertySection1Intro": "Puberty is the developmental stage where the body undergoes rapid hormonal and biological changes, enabling physical maturity and reproductive capability.",
  "pubertySection1Item1": "Hormonal triggers: The pituitary gland releases hormones that signal the body to grow and develop secondary sexual characteristics.",
  "pubertySection1Item2": "Individual timing: Puberty typically begins between ages 9 and 14, varying naturally among individuals.",
  "pubertySection1Item3": "Normal variation: Growing at different rates than peers is completely normal and healthy.",
  "pubertySection2Title": "Physical Changes in Females & Males",
  "pubertySection2Subtitle": "Key Physical Milestones",
  "pubertySection2FemaleTitle": "Changes in Females:",
  "pubertySection2FemaleItem1": "Breast development and widening of hips",
  "pubertySection2FemaleItem2": "Growth of body hair (underarms and pubic area)",
  "pubertySection2FemaleItem3": "Onset of menstruation (menarche), the monthly cycle preparing the uterus for potential pregnancy",
  "pubertySection2MaleTitle": "Changes in Males:",
  "pubertySection2MaleItem1": "Deepening of the voice (voice cracks and lower pitch)",
  "pubertySection2MaleItem2": "Growth of facial, underarm, chest, and pubic hair",
  "pubertySection2MaleItem3": "Growth of testicles and penis, along with nocturnal emissions ('wet dreams'), a normal involuntary release of semen during sleep",
  "pubertySection3Title": "Emotional & Psychological Changes",
  "pubertySection3Subtitle": "Navigating Thoughts and Emotions",
  "pubertySection3Intro": "Alongside physical transformations, teenagers experience significant cognitive and emotional growth.",
  "pubertySection3Item1": "Mood shifts: Fluctuating hormones combined with social adjustments can cause temporary mood swings.",
  "pubertySection3Item2": "Identity search: Developing self-awareness, exploring personal values, and discovering individuality.",
  "pubertySection3Item3": "Desire for independence: Transitioning toward greater personal autonomy and decision-making.",
  "pubertySection3Item4": "Peer influence: Friendships and social groups become central to emotional support and communication.",
  "pubertySection4Title": "Hygiene & Self-Care during Adolescence",
  "pubertySection4Subtitle": "Daily Habits for Lifelong Health",
  "pubertySection4Item1": "Bathing and deodorants: Sweat glands become more active; daily washing with clean water and mild soap prevents body odor.",
  "pubertySection4Item2": "Skin and face care: Increased oil production can cause acne; wash the face twice daily and avoid squeezing pimples.",
  "pubertySection4Item3": "Menstrual hygiene: Change sanitary pads or clean supplies every 4–6 hours, wash hands before and after, and dispose of materials safely.",
  "pubertySection4Item4": "Nutrition and sleep: Growing bodies require 8–10 hours of restful sleep each night and a balanced diet rich in vitamins and iron.",
  "pubertyQuizTitle": "Adolescent Health & Puberty Quiz",
  "pubertyQ1Text": "Puberty is a natural biological transition where each person grows and develops at their own unique, normal pace.",
  "pubertyQ1Insight": "Puberty begins at different times for everyone, usually between ages 9 and 14. Variance in timing and growth rate is completely normal.",
  "pubertyQ2Text": "What is a normal and healthy physical change that occurs during male puberty?",
  "pubertyQ2OptA": "Deepening of the voice and growth of facial/body hair",
  "pubertyQ2OptB": "Sudden complete loss of appetite",
  "pubertyQ2OptC": "Permanent loss of sleep",
  "pubertyQ2OptD": "Inability to run or exercise",
  "pubertyQ2Insight": "Voice deepening, growth spurts, and hair development are classic signs of testosterone production and healthy male pubertal development.",
  "pubertyQ3Text": "What is the recommended hygiene practice during menstruation?",
  "pubertyQ3OptA": "Change sanitary products regularly (every 4–6 hours) and wash hands",
  "pubertyQ3OptB": "Avoid bathing until the cycle ends",
  "pubertyQ3OptC": "Use unwashed cloths repeatedly without drying",
  "pubertyQ3OptD": "Avoid drinking water throughout the cycle",
  "pubertyQ3Insight": "Regularly changing sanitary supplies every 4 to 6 hours and practicing good hand hygiene prevents irritation and bacterial infections.",
  "pubertyQ4Text": "Emotional changes and mood shifts during adolescence are common and can be managed through open communication, exercise, and adequate sleep.",
  "pubertyQ4Insight": "Hormonal shifts and expanding social worlds make emotional fluctuations normal. Talking to trusted friends or family helps navigate these feelings.",
  "stiQuizTitle": "Sexually Transmitted Infections Quiz",
  "stiQ1Text": "Many sexually transmitted infections (STIs) may show no noticeable symptoms at first, which is why regular screening and testing are essential.",
  "stiQ1Insight": "Asymptomatic infections are common with STIs like chlamydia and gonorrhea. Regular testing allows prompt treatment before complications occur.",
  "stiQ2Text": "Which of the following is an effective method for reducing the risk of transmitting most STIs during sexual activity?",
  "stiQ2OptA": "Consistent and correct use of male or female condoms",
  "stiQ2OptB": "Taking vitamins immediately before contact",
  "stiQ2OptC": "Washing with hot water after intercourse only",
  "stiQ2OptD": "Relying on herbal teas",
  "stiQ2Insight": "Condoms provide a physical barrier that drastically lowers the risk of STI transmission when used correctly every time.",
  "stiQ3Text": "If you test positive for a bacterial STI and start antibiotics, what is the most important instruction to follow?",
  "stiQ3OptA": "Complete the entire course of medication as prescribed by the healthcare worker",
  "stiQ3OptB": "Stop medication the moment pain disappears",
  "stiQ3OptC": "Share the remaining pills with friends",
  "stiQ3OptD": "Double the dose on the final day",
  "stiQ3Insight": "Stopping antibiotics early can lead to bacterial resistance and reinfection. Always complete the entire prescribed treatment course.",
  "stiQ4Text": "When someone is diagnosed and treated for an STI, it is vital that sexual partners are also notified and treated to prevent reinfection.",
  "stiQ4Insight": "Treating partners simultaneously breaks the cycle of transmission and protects both individuals from repeated health complications.",
  "hepatitisQuizTitle": "Hepatitis Module Quiz",
  "hepatitisQ1Text": "Hepatitis primarily affects which vital organ in the human body?",
  "hepatitisQ1OptA": "Liver",
  "hepatitisQ1OptB": "Lungs",
  "hepatitisQ1OptC": "Heart",
  "hepatitisQ1OptD": "Kidneys",
  "hepatitisQ1Insight": "Hepatitis is an inflammation of the liver, which performs vital roles like filtering toxins, aiding digestion, and storing energy.",
  "hepatitisQ2Text": "Hepatitis A and Hepatitis E are primarily spread through contaminated food and water, making clean water and hand hygiene key defenses.",
  "hepatitisQ2Insight": "Hepatitis A and E spread via the fecal-oral route. Thorough handwashing and safe drinking water effectively stop transmission.",
  "hepatitisQ3Text": "Which safe and effective preventive measure protects against Hepatitis B infection?",
  "hepatitisQ3OptA": "Hepatitis B vaccination and screening",
  "hepatitisQ3OptB": "Drinking caffeinated beverages",
  "hepatitisQ3OptC": "Avoiding regular exercise",
  "hepatitisQ3OptD": "Taking painkillers daily",
  "hepatitisQ3Insight": "The Hepatitis B vaccine provides long-lasting immunity. Avoiding needle-sharing and safe sexual practices also prevent transmission.",
  "hepatitisQ4Text": "Modern direct-acting antiviral (DAA) medications can successfully cure more than 95% of chronic Hepatitis C infections.",
  "hepatitisQ4Insight": "Advances in medicine have made chronic Hepatitis C a curable condition with an oral medication course lasting 8 to 12 weeks.",
  "gbvQuizTitle": "Gender-Based Violence Quiz",
  "gbvQ1Text": "Gender-based violence (GBV) includes physical, sexual, psychological, and economic harm directed at an individual based on their gender.",
  "gbvQ1Insight": "GBV encompasses all forms of coercive control, emotional abuse, physical harm, and economic deprivation rooted in unequal gender norms.",
  "gbvQ2Text": "What is a vital immediate step when supporting a survivor who has experienced sexual violence?",
  "gbvQ2OptA": "Ensure their safety and seek confidential medical care (including PEP within 72 hours)",
  "gbvQ2OptB": "Blame them for being in the situation",
  "gbvQ2OptC": "Disclose their private story to neighborhood groups",
  "gbvQ2OptD": "Ignore their feelings and tell them to stay silent",
  "gbvQ2Insight": "Immediate safety, non-judgmental empathy, and clinical care—including post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP) within 72 hours—are crucial for survivors.",
  "gbvQ3Text": "What role can men and boys play in preventing gender-based violence in our communities?",
  "gbvQ3OptA": "Promote respectful relationships, reject violent norms, and speak out against abuse",
  "gbvQ3OptB": "Remain silent when witnessing harassment",
  "gbvQ3OptC": "Enforce rigid traditional inequalities",
  "gbvQ3OptD": "Discourage girls from attending school",
  "gbvQ3Insight": "Engaging men and boys as allies and champions for gender equality creates safer, more supportive communities for everyone.",
  "gbvQ4Text": "Survivors of GBV are never to blame for the violence committed against them.",
  "gbvQ4Insight": "Responsibility for violence rests solely on the perpetrator. Blaming survivors causes secondary trauma and deters people from seeking help.",
  "srhHeroDesc": "Sexual and Reproductive Health is a state of physical, mental, and social well-being in all matters relating to the reproductive system, relationships, and fertility.",
  "srhNoteText": "Informed decisions, mutual respect, open communication, and access to healthcare ensure healthy relationships and empowered family planning.",
  "srhSection1Title": "Understanding Sexual & Reproductive Health",
  "srhSection1Subtitle": "A Holistic View of Wellbeing",
  "srhSection1Intro": "SRH is not just about avoiding disease; it is the right to make informed choices about your body, sexuality, and future.",
  "srhSection1Item1": "Physical health: Caring for reproductive organs, preventing infections, and accessing routine checkups.",
  "srhSection1Item2": "Emotional health: Developing positive self-esteem, healthy body image, and respect in intimate relationships.",
  "srhSection1Item3": "Social wellbeing: Experiencing equality, free from stigma, coercion, or gender discrimination.",
  "srhSection2Title": "Anatomy & Reproductive Systems",
  "srhSection2Subtitle": "How the Human Body Functions",
  "srhSection2Intro": "Understanding how female and male reproductive systems work empowers individuals to care for their bodily health.",
  "srhSection2Item1": "Female anatomy: Ovaries produce eggs and hormones (estrogen/progesterone); fallopian tubes guide eggs to the uterus where a baby can develop.",
  "srhSection2Item2": "Male anatomy: Testicles produce sperm and testosterone; sperm travels through the vas deferens and urethra.",
  "srhSection2Item3": "Conception: Fertilization occurs when a sperm cell joins with a mature egg, initiating pregnancy.",
  "srhSection3Title": "Family Planning & Contraceptive Methods",
  "srhSection3Subtitle": "Empowering Family and Life Planning",
  "srhSection3Intro": "Family planning allows individuals and couples to decide if and when to have children, promoting maternal and child health.",
  "srhSection3Item1": "Barrier methods: Male and female condoms prevent pregnancy and provide essential protection against STIs and HIV (dual protection).",
  "srhSection3Item2": "Hormonal methods: Pills, injections, implants, and IUDs offer reliable pregnancy prevention by regulating ovulation and cervical mucus.",
  "srhSection3Item3": "Permanent methods: Vasectomy and tubal ligation are voluntary surgical procedures for individuals who have completed their family size.",
  "srhSection3Item4": "Emergency contraception: Can prevent unintended pregnancy when taken within 72 to 120 hours following unprotected intercourse.",
  "srhSection4Title": "Consent, Rights & Healthy Relationships",
  "srhSection4Subtitle": "Building Trust and Mutual Respect",
  "srhSection4Intro": "Healthy relationships are built on communication, equality, and uncoerced consent.",
  "srhSection4Item1": "What is consent: An active, freely given, reversible, and informed agreement between partners.",
  "srhSection4Item2": "Bodily autonomy: Everyone has the fundamental right to make decisions regarding their own body and reproductive health.",
  "srhSection4Item3": "Effective communication: Talking openly with partners about boundaries, health history, and contraception leads to safer relationships.",
  "srhQuizTitle": "Sexual & Reproductive Health Quiz",
  "srhQ1Text": "Sexual and Reproductive Health includes physical, emotional, and social well-being, not merely the absence of disease.",
  "srhQ1Insight": "The World Health Organization recognizes SRH as a positive, holistic state of wellbeing that respects individual rights and choices.",
  "srhQ2Text": "Which family planning method provides 'dual protection' by preventing both unintended pregnancy AND sexually transmitted infections?",
  "srhQ2OptA": "External or internal condoms",
  "srhQ2OptB": "Daily oral contraceptive pills alone",
  "srhQ2OptC": "Injectable hormones alone",
  "srhQ2OptD": "Intrauterine devices (IUDs) alone",
  "srhQ2Insight": "While hormonal methods prevent pregnancy effectively, only barrier methods like condoms protect against STIs and HIV simultaneously.",
  "srhQ3Text": "Consent in any healthy relationship must be freely given, enthusiastic, informed, and can be changed or withdrawn at any time.",
  "srhQ3Insight": "True consent requires freedom of choice without pressure or fear. Respecting partner boundaries is fundamental to healthy relationships.",
  "srhQ4Text": "Family planning enables individuals and couples to choose if, when, and how many children to have, supporting maternal and family well-being.",
  "srhQ4Insight": "Family planning empowers women and families, reduces maternal complications, and supports healthy child development and financial stability."
}
